  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "Nobody knew how old the 180-foot man on the hill was. Dorset, England. Cut into white chalk, a 121-foot club in hand.",
        "visual_query": "dorset chalk hillside england",
        "visual_fallback": "green hillside"
      },
      {
        "narration": "The earliest written mention is a churchwarden's note from November 1694: three shillings, for repairing ye Giant. Before that, the record says nothing at all.",
        "visual_query": "english parish church interior",
        "visual_fallback": "church interior"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Iron Age Celts? A 17th-century landowner's joke? For 300 years the guesses ranged across two thousand years of history. Then someone tested the soil itself.",
        "visual_query": "rolling english downs green",
        "visual_fallback": "english countryside"
      },
      {
        "narration": "In 2021 the National Trust dated grains of chalk by optically stimulated luminescence. The answer: late Saxon. Somewhere between 700 and 1100 AD.",
        "visual_query": "chalk soil close up",
        "visual_fallback": "white chalk"
      },
      {
        "narration": "A 2024 study goes further. It may be Hercules, cut as a muster point where West Saxon armies gathered. A road sign for an army, 180 feet tall.",
        "visual_query": "anglo saxon reenactment field",
        "visual_fallback": "grass field"
      }
    ],
